  "query_preview": "..\n\nKEY MESSAGES:\n\n-  Rain in northwest Montana diminishes by this evening. \n\n-  A brief drying and warming trend is expected Wednesday and\n   Thursday. Fog or low clouds are possible each morning.\n\n-  Chance for frost in high valleys of southwest Montana.\n\n-  Unsettled with showers on Friday. Growing confidence for a wet\n   and cool system with high elevation snow developing by this\n   weekend\n\n\nSatellite imagery this afternoon shows a trough of low pressure \nmoving east over the Continental Divide. This system brought \ncopious rainfall to much of the Northern Rockies over the Labor \nDay weekend. Wraparound moisture from this low will continue to\nsupply light rain over the Flathead Valley and the Glacier Park \nregion this afternoon.  Precipitation rates will diminish going \ninto this evening. \n\nIn the wake of the low, upper level flow will turn \nnorthwesterly by Wednesday. As it does, a tightening pressure \ngradient and upper level jet streak approaching the Northern \nRockies will bring an increase in westerly winds for Wednesday and\nThursday. Expect choppy conditions on area lakes including \nFlathead Lake both days. A broad trough will traverse the\nCanadian border on Thursday, pushing a cold front through the \nNorthern Rockies, further enhancing westerly winds. Drier air \naccompanying increase winds will create elevated fire weather \nconcerns for southwest Montana on Thursday. Daytime high \ntemperatures for Wednesday and Thursday will bounce back to near \nseasonal norms. \n\nMore unsettled weather is on the way for the weekend. Model\nensembles are on board for low pressure to return. The broad\ntrough along the border will bring some chances for precipitation\non Friday, then a secondary, deeper trough arrives on Saturday \nthrough Sunday. While ensemble solutions like the change to \nunsettled weather, actual precipitation amounts are still up in \nthe air. Regardless of precipitation amounts, this system does \nlook cold enough for precipitation to fall in the form of snow in\nthe high elevations (7,000 - 8,000 feet), especially along the \nContinental Divide including Glacier National Park.",
